    "content": "# How to Create a GST-Compliant Invoice for Interior Projects\n\nA non-compliant invoice is worse than no invoice — your client cannot claim input tax credit, will hold payment, and you face penalty risk. This is the exact step-by-step to issue a GST-compliant tax invoice for an interior project in India.\n\n## Step 1: Confirm the trigger\n\nYou issue a tax invoice when:\n\n- A milestone is hit (advance, dispatch, installation, handover).\n- A service is delivered (design fee, supervision sign-off).\n- Goods leave your premises.\n\nIssue within 30 days of service completion. For goods, at the time of removal/dispatch.\n\n## Step 2: Pick the invoice number\n\nThe invoice number must be:\n\n- **Unique** within the financial year.\n- **Sequential** — no gaps.\n- **Max 16 characters**, alphanumeric, plus / and -.\n\nFormat example: `INT/2026-27/0042`.\n\nNever edit a number after issuing. If you cancel, issue a credit note (not a number reuse).\n\n## Step 3: Identify Place of Supply\n\n- Installation work: site address state.\n- Pure design (delivered remotely): client's registered address.\n- Goods dispatched to site: site address state.\n\nThis determines CGST+SGST vs IGST. Get it wrong and your client cannot claim ITC.\n\n## Step 4: Build the line items\n\nEach row needs:\n\n- Description with specification.\n- HSN (for goods) or SAC (for services).\n- Quantity and unit.\n- Rate.\n- Taxable value.\n- Tax rate.\n\nSee [GST rates for interior work](/blog/gst-rates-interior-design-india) for codes.\n\n## Step 5: Compute taxes correctly\n\n- **Intra-state:** Taxable value × 9% = CGST; same × 9% = SGST.\n- **Inter-state:** Taxable value × 18% = IGST.\n\nRound at the row level, then sum. Some accountants prefer rounding only at totals — pick one rule and apply consistently.\n\n## Step 6: Include the mandatory header block\n\n- Your name, address, GSTIN, PAN.\n- Recipient name, address, GSTIN.\n- Place of supply with state code.\n- Reverse charge applicability.\n- Invoice date.\n\n## Step 7: Add the footer block\n\n- Subtotal, discount, taxable value, tax breakup, grand total.\n- Amount in words.\n- Bank details (for direct transfer).\n- Terms (payment due, late fees).\n- Authorised signatory.\n\n## Step 8: Generate the PDF and share\n\nSave as PDF with a clean filename: `INT-2026-27-0042-ClientName.pdf`. Email plus WhatsApp link.\n\n## Step 9: Record in your books\n\nUpdate your GST register the same day. At month end, your GSTR-1 should pull directly from this register — no last-minute typing.\n\n## Step 10: Hold the cancellation discipline\n\nIf you must cancel, issue a credit note referencing the original invoice number. Never delete or renumber.\n\n## Where designers go wrong\n\n- **Reusing draft invoice numbers.** Audit failure.\n- **Place of supply = client billing address for site work.** ITC denied.\n- **Bundled tax shown only at footer, no per-row split.** Client cannot reconcile.\n- **No digital signature on the PDF.** Corporate clients reject.\n\n## Doing it in 60 seconds with software\n\nThe 10 steps above collapse to a single conversion in Intorza:\n\n1. Convert approved quotation → tax invoice.\n2. Confirm milestone.\n3. Hit Generate.\n\nGSTIN, HSN/SAC, place-of-supply logic, sequential numbering, and signing are all automatic.\n\n[Start free →](https://app.intorza.com)\n\n## Related reading\n\n- [GST invoice for interior designers in India — complete guide](/blog/gst-invoice-interior-designers-india-guide)\n- [GST rates for interior design and furniture work](/blog/gst-rates-interior-design-india)\n- [HSN & SAC codes every interior designer should know](/blog/hsn-sac-codes-interior-designers)\n"
