    "content": "# How to Manage Interior Design Teams and Vendors Efficiently\n\nA 10-person interior studio that delivers 30 projects a year does not run on talent alone — it runs on rituals, roles, and a vendor network that performs predictably. Here is the playbook.\n\n## Roles inside the studio\n\nA typical mid-size studio has:\n\n- **Principal designer** — concept, client relationship, final approvals.\n- **Senior designer** — design ownership per project.\n- **Junior designer / draftsman** — CAD, drawings, BOQs.\n- **Project manager (PM)** — execution timeline, vendor coordination, cash flow per project.\n- **Site supervisor** — physical site presence, daily reporting.\n- **Account / admin** — invoicing, payments, GST, vendor PO.\n\nIn a 3-person studio, one person wears 3 hats. The hats still need to exist mentally. When you cannot say which hat you are wearing at a given moment, decisions blur.\n\n## The four weekly rituals\n\n### Monday — Project Standup (30 min)\nEach PM walks through every active project: stage, blockers, this-week deliverables. No discussion of solutions — just visibility.\n\n### Wednesday — Design Review (60 min)\nAll new designs that have crossed concept stage are reviewed by the principal. Catches direction issues before they hit production.\n\n### Friday — Cash Review (30 min)\nInvoices issued, payments received, payments due. Vendor payments scheduled for the following week.\n\n### Daily — 5-minute site WhatsApp update\nEach site supervisor sends 5 photos and 3 lines (planned / done / blocked) every evening. No exceptions.\n\nThese four rituals replace 30 ad-hoc messages a day.\n\n## Vendor management — the only system that works\n\n### Maintain a master vendor list\n\nPer vendor:\n\n- Category (modular, civil, electrical, painting, plumbing, etc.).\n- Owner contact.\n- Payment terms.\n- Lead time (typical).\n- On-time %.\n- Defect %.\n- Notes (strengths, weaknesses).\n\n### Score them quarterly\n\nUse a 1–5 scale on three vectors: quality, on-time delivery, communication. Drop bottom-quartile vendors after two quarters of poor scores. This is the fastest way to improve studio output.\n\n### Two-vendor rule\n\nFor every critical category, maintain at least two qualified vendors. Single-vendor dependence is a delivery risk.\n\n### Payment discipline\n\nPay vendors on agreed terms. Studios that delay vendor payment by 30 days lose vendor priority — you become the third-priority client, your timelines slip, your client blames you.\n\n### Purchase orders, not WhatsApp messages\n\nEvery order goes through a written PO with: item spec, quantity, rate, delivery date, payment terms. WhatsApp orders create disputes.\n\n## Communication discipline\n\n- **One channel per project** — WhatsApp group with client, principal, PM, site supervisor. Nothing else.\n- **No design changes over voice calls.** Always confirmed in writing.\n- **Weekly client report** — bullet update + photos every Friday. Predictable communication kills 90% of anxiety-driven calls.\n\n## Escalation paths\n\nWhen something breaks:\n\n1. PM tries to resolve within 24 hours.\n2. If unresolved, escalates to principal with options (A/B/C).\n3. Principal decides within 48 hours.\n4. Client is informed of the resolution path, not the problem.\n\nClients are never the first to know there is a problem. They are the first to know there is a solution.\n\n## Building bench strength\n\n- Cross-train at least one PM into design review.\n- Cross-train at least one designer into procurement.\n- Document every recurring process in a 1-page SOP.\n\nWhen your best PM is on leave, projects should not stall.\n\n## Common team/vendor mistakes\n\n- **No daily site report.** Surprises at month-end.\n- **Heroic individual carrying the studio.** Single point of failure.\n- **Vendor list lives in one person's phone.** Continuity collapses when they leave.\n- **No vendor scoring.** Bad vendors stay because no one tracks them.\n\n## Tools that pay back fast\n\n- Shared project tracker (Notion, ClickUp, or built into your studio software).\n- A vendor scorecard sheet (Google Sheets is fine).\n- Quotation + invoicing system (so accounts is not the bottleneck).\n\nIntorza covers the quotation, invoicing, and project-tracking layer for interior studios. [Try it free →](https://app.intorza.com)\n\n## What to do next\n\nPick the weakest of the four weekly rituals above. Run it for 4 consecutive weeks. Output improvement compounds quickly.\n\nRelated: [interior design project management workflow](/blog/interior-design-project-management-guide).\n"
